diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index 02ae15a..71ac784 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -37,6 +37,10 @@ Each run has an experiment name (`experiment` in the config). Its output goes to
 
 To continue a run from its latest checkpoint, set `restart = true` and run again with the same experiment name. The run still ends at `end_date`.
 
+While a run goes, its output folder holds `RUNNING` (which host and Julia process write there; a second run of the same experiment stops at once instead of overwriting the files) and `status.txt` (state, model day, wall minutes per model day, expected end; its last three lines are the state, the progress and the time of the update, so `tail -n 3 output/*/status.txt` lists every run). Runs can follow each other in one Julia REPL: `include("panarctic.jl")` clears the previous run's globals and GPU memory first (`run_management.jl`).
+
+With `post_run_analysis = true` in `[output]`, the run ends with its scorecard: the model is cleared from memory, every analysis that applies runs (salinity drift, sea ice, CATKE mixing, fastest point, gateways against Tsubouchi et al. 2018), and their key rows go to `scorecard.md` in the output folder. For an earlier run, `julia --project=. analysis/scorecard.jl config_X.toml`.
+
 ## Transports
 
 Volume, heat, salt and freshwater transports through Fram Strait, the Barents Sea Opening, Bering Strait and Davis Strait (Sv, TW, kg/s, Sv; positive into the Arctic), and through the two open boundaries (`atlantic_boundary`, `pacific_boundary`; positive into the domain). The sections are defined in `[transports]` in the config as longitude/latitude waypoints, so they don't depend on the grid: the faces they cross are found on whatever grid the run uses. Both ends of a section should be on land; a warning says when one isn't. Heat and freshwater are relative to `reference_temperature` and `reference_salinity`.
